      <section data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h3 id="why-bring-ai-into-the-browser">Turning the request into actions</h3>
        <p>The agent reads the request alongside the page it can see, using both to decide which action would move the task forward. After each action, it reads the resulting page before continuing. This is where AI does useful work, interpreting a colleague’s intent in the terms of the application and adjusting as the task develops.</p>
        <p>If a sign-in screen appears, the agent waits for the operator to complete it. If the operator edits the form, it continues with the revised page. I kept the ticket itself as the starting point because asking someone to translate their request into a detailed setup form would give them back much of the work they were trying to delegate.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h3 id="choosing-an-architecture-people-can-work-with">Why a browser extension?</h3>
        <p>The operator already has a browser with access to the systems they work in. I wanted AI to work there with as little extra setup as possible. The choice of architecture would determine where the task ran, what the operator had to install, and how they could step in.</p>
        <BrowserArchitectureComparison />
        <p>A browser extension is a small add-on installed in Chrome or Edge. It gave me one place to put both browser control and the interaction cards. The same extension that prepares a change in the application can ask the operator to approve it beside the work. Sign-in and MFA happen in that familiar browser whenever they are needed.</p>
        <p>I kept reasoning and task state in the cloud so the agent could be maintained centrally. The operator installs the extension once and starts tickets from the dashboard, with no local automation service to configure.</p>
      </section>

