      <section class="capability-opening project-conclusion" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h2 id="where-judgement-changes-hands" data-source-anchor="where-judgement-changes-hands">Where judgement changes hands</h2>
        <p class="chapter-intro">Reasoning, structured guidance, deterministic execution, and human responsibility across the same workflow.</p>
        <p>Building these two parts made me see the boundary as something that moves with the kind of uncertainty in front of the system. Early in a search, nobody knows which wording will uncover the best documentation. During a browser task, the next page may look different from the one the agent expected. These are useful places for AI reasoning because the system has to interpret meaning, connect partial information, and revise a plan as new evidence appears.</p>
        <p>In Knowledge Management, that reasoning begins well before a crawler opens a page. AI suggests questions the existing library may be unable to answer, groups related gaps into a shared research objective, and searches for authoritative seed pages. During focused crawling, it judges which links remain relevant, which source is likely to add new information, and when an uncovered question calls for another seed search. The original questions stay in place as coverage requirements, while page limits, time limits, and diminishing information give the exploration a defined boundary.</p>
        <p>Once a source has been chosen, more of the work can become predictable. Extraction, normalisation, deduplication, chunking, and provenance mapping follow the same rules each time. Source monitoring begins in the same way. The system revisits a known URL, extracts its main content, normalises it, and compares it with the accepted snapshot. An unchanged page stops there. When the words have changed, AI returns to judge what the difference means for the knowledge derived from that page.</p>
        <p>Browser Control moves between these modes more frequently. The agent interprets the ticket and the current page, chooses a next action, then receives a fresh observation after Playwright and the browser extension carry out the click or form entry. The action itself is structured and recorded. Deciding which action serves the request depends on reasoning. Sign-in and multi-factor authentication return control to the operator, and a consequential change is prepared for approval before submission. The agent can do the repetitive navigation and adapt to the interface, while a person remains responsible for the point at which the business system is changed.</p>
        <p>These relationships form gradients. AI reasoning still works inside tool schemas, coverage requirements, task history, and operational limits. Deterministic steps still depend on earlier judgements about which source or action deserves to enter them. I used structure to narrow the space of possible decisions as the work moved closer to a lasting consequence. People enter where context, authority, or accountability cannot be delegated safely. This gives the agent room to explore and adapt while keeping the resulting knowledge and actions inspectable.</p>
